      "title": "Calculating a Directional Derivative"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\left(x + 3 y^{2}\\right)^{2}$ at the point $A(1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(3,-4).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,-4⟩-⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}}⟨2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨2,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 3 y^{2}\\right)^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 3 y^{2}\\right)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(x+3y^{2})\\cdot 1,2(x+3y^{2})\\cdot 6y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x + 6 y^{2},12 y \\left(x + 3 y^{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =⟨2 (1) + 6 (-1)^{2},12 (-1) \\left((1) + 3 (-1)^{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-48⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇f(1,-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨8,-48⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{13}}{13}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(8)\\cdot (2)+(-48)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{160 \\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
