    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} y + x e^{y}$ at the point $A(-2,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,-4).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,-4⟩-⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}}⟨2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{5}}⟨2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} y + x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} y + x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 x y + e^{y},x \\left(3 x + e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-2,0) & =⟨6 (-2) (0) + e^{(0)},3 (-2)^{2} + (-2) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(-2,0) & =∇f(-2,0)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨1,10⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =- \\frac{19 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
