    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} y + 4 x e^{y}$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(4,2).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨4,2⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨3,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨3,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} y + 4 x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} y + 4 x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x y + 4 e^{y},x \\left(x + 4 e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =⟨2 (1) (0) + 4 e^{(0)},(1)^{2} + 4 (1) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{13}}{13},\\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{22 \\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
